Dude, you never got one? That's so weird.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.680
Lucky. All right.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:35.480
So let's talk about Puzznik because there is a whole other game in here too, which is weird.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.600
But like, let's just talk about Puzznik for a second.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:42.480
There are 16 stages, 10 puzzles in each of these stages.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:45.720
So it's kind of like a 1-1, 1-2 kind of deal.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:49.520
So clearing one particular puzzle doesn't make you a genius.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:52.520
You have to clear all 10 to kind of successfully clear the stage.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.600
It makes 160 stages in total.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.260
So there's certainly a lot of game here.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:00.680
You clear every piece by matching like symbols and two or more combos.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:03.400
But you have to do it before the timer hits zero.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:10.920
And the timer is pretty aggressive in this game in the sense of like when more things happen on the screen.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:15.440
Like I do agree that they ease you into it because you get, what is it, usually 90 seconds?

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:18.620
No, it depends on the size of the level, right?

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:20.140
Yeah, I've seen 70. I've seen 60.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.580
Okay. So then, but it's still mostly forgiving early on.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:30.600
But later on, when you have to actually think about what you're going to do, that clock is probably the most intimidating part of the game.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:39.480
It's not just like, sure, given an infinite time scale, you could move these pieces around and figure out how to clear them so that you clear all of them.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:40.960
Because again, that's the hook, right?

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.900
It's not just about matching symbols together all the time.

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:45.380
You have to make sure that you clear everything.

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:46.840
You can't leave a piece behind.

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:54.040
So if there are strategically three symbols of the same block, you can't clear two of them because now you'll be stuck with one.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:55.900
And, you know, that's a fail state.

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:56.960
So you'll have to start over.

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:09.160
The matching, though, in general, the real thing behind it, right, is that there's placement of the remainder of the screen so that certain blocks can't be moved.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:14.240
Or there are elevators that kind of move up and down the screen to transport blocks.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:17.000
There are blocks that have to fall into other blocks.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:26.360
It's the design of the specific level that makes it maybe a little bit hard to talk about this game in like a as a dialogue discourse.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:33.460
But ultimately, that's the interesting hook of the game is the is the setup and then you having to solve it from that.

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:38.260
Right. They're always giving you they're kind of leading you with the way that it's set up.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:43.560
Yeah, it's very much like an order of operations sort of puzzle.

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.180
I guess a lot of most puzzles are can be boiled down to that.

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:57.420
But it's very important here because you can very easily like soft lock a puzzle within like the first move.

00:09:57.580 --> 00:09:59.660
And then you have to use a retry.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:02.120
And that's not any fun.

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:04.280
But, yeah, it's a little.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:14.000
But then there's also like some twitch reflex going on here, too, because of that, like movement and the fact that it's gravity based.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.400
And sometimes you need to like move things while gravity is acting upon something else.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:25.600
It's it's not as intense as that might sound, but it it can get a little tricky.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:30.380
I'm curious what you guys think about my take on this.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:33.080
But I think that this doesn't need the timer.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.120
I get that it's like for that, like excitement or whatever.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:38.680
I think that this is these are fine puzzles without the timer.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:41.680
You're just going to keep retrying anyways when the time runs out.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:45.540
So I don't know really what the timer adds personally.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:50.160
I just want to look at the puzzle until I solve it with a game like this.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:51.660
I know that that might not be the.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:55.180
I agree. Like the intent of the of the developer.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:11:00.660
But like to me, whenever there's a game like this and there's a timer and it feels unnecessary, I just I don't know.

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:06.560
They had something here where it was like, oh, this is different enough from Tetris because it's not about like, oh, my God, what's going to happen?

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:07.480
What where can I put this?

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:10.380
You're not panicking. It's more like let's sit and think about this puzzle.

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.300
And then they added the timer to kind of add that back in anyways.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:14.960
Yeah, I agree.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:19.340
Because like, again, you can fail without running out of time.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:20.900
I guess isn't something right yet.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:24.680
Like, oh, it's all about like just experimenting.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:28.380
And and like, no, if you do something wrong, you're screwed.

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:30.620
So. Right. I think you're right.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:32.140
Yeah, I would agree, too.

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:38.100
I don't think I can think of in their defense is that the, you know, 160 total puzzles.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:41.660
The timer is designed to make these kind of quick bursts.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:45.260
But that doesn't really matter if you wind up failing them anyway.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:46.740
Like it's just going to take longer.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:56.200
The game is incredibly forgiving, though, when it comes to retrying and starting over, because the only thing that happens is your score resets.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:12:01.760
When you run out of retries, you don't like start back at the beginning of the stage you're on.

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:03.900
You just start right where you are and move forward.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:15.080
I guess that that sort of balances it out, because if maybe the only reason you would care about the score is if you're trying to do it fast, like, you know.

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:19.840
Right. And the amount of time you have left is what, you know, boost your score the most.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:22.500
So it's like all twitch reflex stuff.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:25.840
Right. Because I've never solved a ribbix cube in my life.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:30.800
But the people that do do that tend to have some pride with how fast they do it.

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:33.660
So, yeah, that's what I'm going to go with.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:48.000
Now, this game doesn't have exactly that bejeweled candy crush thing that we were talking about, where, you know, there's like a cascade where you switch two blocks and they clear, but then more blocks fall down.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:55.380
So they clear things that you didn't know, you know, like and all of a sudden it's like, wow, that caused a chain reaction of match threes and then a match four.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:57.860
And then, you know, now there's these new symbols popping up.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:05.980
It's not really this game, but you can design this game where, like, you match certain things to create other matches as well.

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:08.520
What's your play style here?

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:16.860
Are you, before you're even, like, before you're making moves, are you kind of counting the pairs and thinking about, like, where's the best piece to start?

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:27.760
Or are you just more of a, like, feel the movement, like, you know, solve the first pair in front of you and then deal with the next one and so on and so forth?

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:29.240
Just vibe it out?

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:30.400
Yeah, exactly.

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:38.560
When I started, it was more so just vibe it out, just move stuff around, it'll all start to click.

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:47.080
Then once I started to get those, like, straggler blocks, I was like, okay, I gotta actually, like, try something here.

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:50.360
So I did start counting, like, oh, do any of these have odd numbers?

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:58.920
Or are they sort of grouped in a way that you're going to have to do, like, a couple three-in-ones?

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:10.880
And, yeah, over time, once you start to learn the ways that the game is going to try and trick you, you become a bit more cognizant of it.

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:19.480
Yeah, I like to usually, like, try to just, you know, go with my gut on a first attempt.

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:29.800
And then on a second attempt, if I failed, I look at how I failed, and I also, like, really will pull back now and, like, look at, okay, if I put this here, like, where is everything going to line up?

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:35.200
Where am I going to accidentally put two things against each other that I don't want to disappear just yet?

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:44.480
And, like, I really start thinking a few steps ahead, but I do usually, because there's no real punishment for getting it wrong, I do usually try to go intuitively at first.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:52.660
Which surprisingly works more often than you think, because I do feel like the game definitely gets more and more difficult, but I don't feel like it's a perfectly linear difficulty.

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:57.260
I feel like sometimes I'm like, okay, now we're getting difficult, and then I get, like, a real, like, lob ball in there.

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:01.000
Like, just that's super easy, and then another hard one, and then, like, one that's a little easier.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:12.060
So sometimes I can just kind of solve it intuitively, and then when I fail those ones, that's when I'm like, all right, really sit back and, like, think out my moves before I actually make them.

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:19.100
But, like, when you do fail, like, do you have that moment of, like, goddammit, Joe, you're so stupid, like, how would you think that, Joe?

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:21.500
And then you just sort of beat yourself up for an hour or so?

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:24.620
Yeah, but I'm normally, like, thinking that way about myself at all times.

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:25.860
Oh, okay. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:26.960
Just making sure.

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:29.360
For the record. Just kidding, everybody.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:31.700
No need to call any, like, support lines.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:32.960
Can we get a check on Joe?

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:51.280
Yeah. And then, I think, Sean, I think you were alluding to this earlier, but this is something I'm calling temporary pairs, where, and this feels like the only part of the game that, I think, like, at a, sooner than later, it is necessary, but I feel like it's also wrong.

00:15:51.280 --> 00:16:01.400
There's this ability to create a pair and then just drop the third one into place to cache all three at once, even though they didn't all, like, match at the same time.

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:05.700
Like, you're given, like, a few frames to kind of, like, get that third piece in there.

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:07.880
And that just doesn't feel right.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:11.240
Like, I'm happy I solved the puzzle, but it doesn't feel like I correctly solved it.

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:12.160
Now, wait a minute, though.

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:13.660
Well, that's designed.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:24.420
And I think, unless you're telling me otherwise, that it's only, like, yeah, the pieces didn't fall into place, but they were still as a result of your same last move.

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:29.080
It's not like you can make a move and then quickly make another move before something falls into place, right?

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:31.540
You kind of do have to do that.

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:44.840
Like, in some of these levels, the, like, there, you have to use, like, an elevator to move some, to move one piece into place that would then make contact with the second piece.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:52.560
But in order to get the third piece in there, you then have to move that as well while the other one is on the elevator.

00:16:52.560 --> 00:17:01.640
And so, like, there's some Twitch reflex stuff going on, and it's not entirely great with the way that the controls work because your cursor is super sticky.

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:07.340
And, like, it's like, I don't know.

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:08.360
